</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Hyperparameter Impact Analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the sensitivity of HyperAdaLoRA’s NLG performance to the orthogonality regularization coefficient <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. We finetune LLaMA3.1-8B with <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> values set to <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.1,0.15,0.2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.1,0.15,0.2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.5 Hyperparameter Impact Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ HyperAdaLoRA: Accelerating LoRA Rank Allocation During Training via Hypernetworks without Sacrificing Performance" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, performance remains relatively stable across the tested <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> values. Although <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> yields slightly better results in this specific setup, the differences are minimal. This indicates that HyperAdaLoRA is robust to variations in this hyperparameter, thereby simplifying its practical application.</p>
</div>
